# previous:  ecd4341 (two work queues kept offering retired engagements).
# includes:  4 commits — R56, a production-readiness round.
#
#            R55 fixed four readers that build their own WHERE over
#            `valuations` and so never inherited `archived_at IS NULL`: the
#            search box, the firm console, the reviewer queue and the pay-now
#            list. It did not establish that those were all of them. They were
#            not. This round enumerated every file that selects from or joins
#            `valuations` and classified each: does this surface ask somebody to
#            act, or is it a record of something that happened?
#
#            Three of the remaining leaks turned out to be worse than any R55
#            found, because they do not render a page — they send mail, and mail
#            leaves the building and cannot be un-sent:
#
#            094fd93 — `dueCandidates` carried neither soft delete, so the drip
#              campaigns kept nudging clients about retired engagements on a
#              cadence, and kept writing to deactivated accounts. It is the only
#              query in the path: nothing between it and `transport.send`
#              re-checks. Also the first `users.deleted_at` leak found outside
#              the search box.
#
#            712efd3 — archiving does not disable a monitor, so the scan kept
#              evaluating retired engagements and emailing the reviewer
#              "Consider a fresh valuation" about withdrawn work.
#
#            32cb490 — archiving does not complete an engagement, so the
#              overdue sweep kept chasing the assigned analyst past SLA. The
#              worst of the three to receive: it repeats every sweep, and the
#              only way to stop it is to finish an engagement that no longer
#              exists.
#
#            162ce75 — the four that only mislead: the two §7.4 remediation
#              queues (whose `count(*) OVER ()` totals were overstated, and
#              whose re-run guard would have recalculated retired work), the
#              inbox with its badge and clear-all, and the `uploads` re-filing
#              queue — where the count did not join `valuations` at all, so
#              filtering only the list would have reproduced the firm console's
#              header-says-15-list-shows-12 gap.
#
#            Each fix goes on the shared fragment both readers use, not in the
#            callers, because two readers disagreeing about what is live is the
#            bug rather than a symptom. Every test file pins a vacuity guard —
#            that archiving leaves `enabled` true, leaves `current_stage`
#            mid-stage — without which the suite would pass on a platform that
#            simply disabled everything on archive.
#
#            Deliberately left, and why. `listPaymentsForScope` (pinned by an
#            R55 test) and `repos/activityLog.ts` are ledgers: money that left a
#            customer's account and events that happened do not stop having done
#            so because the engagement was later retired. `findValuationById`
#            and the bulk-workflow route load by id, which is ops naming a row,
#            not a list offering one. `repos/organizations.ts` (the holding-
#            company rollup) is the one open judgment call — a retired
#            subsidiary arguably overstates a group total, and arguably belongs
#            in its history. Left rather than guessed at.
#

